<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seneca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Onödowaʼga꞉ʼ, Great Hill People, Sonontouans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seneca are an Iroquoian-speaking Indigenous people who historically lived south of Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, the westernmost nation of the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois League), called the Keepers of the Western Door; their own name, Onödowaʼga꞉ʼ, means Great Hill People. The most populous Haudenosaunee nation, about four thousand in the 17th century, they lived in palisaded villages where women owned the land and homes and grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash. Their society is matrilineal: children belong to their mother's clan, and Clan Mothers can depose chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> of the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, they were forced to cede much of their land; over 10,000 Seneca live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-people.jpg" alt="Seneca" width="330" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. Bartoli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornplanter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together they persuaded the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and <b>Seneca</b> to accept the Great Law of Peace, the Gayanashagowa, and finally won over the feared Onondaga chief Tadodaho, whose name became the title of the league’s presiding chief.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It crowned a campaign launched at <b>Seneca</b> Falls in 1848.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A mixed force of Loyalists, British soldiers, <b>Senecas</b> and Mohawks attacked the fort and town of Cherry Valley in central New York, whose defenders were unprepared despite warnings.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
